+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Sparse Background Areas:** Overly large and sparse Gaussian points can significantly affect geometric representations, particularly in background areas [S1].
+*   **Memory Management:** Without dynamic generation and recycling of Gaussians, memory usage can become excessive in large open scenes [S1].
+*   **Subdivision Limits:** Subdivision must stop at a maximum depth to prevent infinite recursion and excessive memory consumption [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering speed is comparable to fast 3DGS rasterization, avoiding the low rendering speed of traditional volume rendering due to continuous sampling [S1]. Training involves MLP evaluations and voxel subdivision, which adds overhead compared to standard 3DGS but is mitigated by sparse grid management.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Significant savings in storage and training memory consumption compared to standard 3DGS. By using a sparse voxel grid and decoding attributes from feature vectors via MLPs, it avoids storing explicit tensors for every Gaussian primitive [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High reconstruction quality, real-time rendering speed, significant savings in storage and training memory, and ability to extract continuous surfaces directly [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Overly large and sparse Gaussian points can still affect geometric representations, particularly in background areas [S1]. Requires careful tuning of subdivision thresholds and pruning periods.
-*   **When to use:** For high-fidelity open scene surface reconstruction where memory efficiency and continuous surface representation are critical.
-*   **When not to use:** For scenes where explicit discrete Gaussian management is sufficient and memory is not a constraint, or for very small scenes where the overhead of voxel management is unnecessary.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Rendering speed is comparable to fast 3DGS rasterization, avoiding the low rendering speed associated with continuous sampling in traditional volume rendering [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Significant savings in storage and training memory consumption compared to explicit 3DGS, due to the implicit continuous representation and sparse voxel grid management [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High reconstruction quality, real-time rendering speed, efficient management of Gaussian splats, and direct mesh extraction capability [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Performance may degrade in background areas if Gaussian points are overly large and sparse [S1]. Requires careful tuning of gradient thresholds and subdivision limits [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Suitable for high-fidelity open scene surface reconstruction where memory efficiency and surface continuity are critical [S1].
+*   **When not to use:** Not ideal for scenes requiring explicit discrete Gaussian primitives for specific artistic or physical effects that rely on the discrete nature of 3DGS [S1].
 
 ## Background
-GVKF was proposed to address the issues of excessive memory consumption and rough surface details in sparse Gaussian areas inherent in discrete 3DGS representations [S1]. It was introduced in the paper "GVKF: Gaussian Voxel Kernel Functions for Highly Efficient Surface Reconstruction in Open Scenes" presented at the 38th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024) [S1]. The method builds on the fast rendering advantages of Gaussian alpha blending while achieving effective implicit scene representation, without the need for computationally intensive volume rendering [S1]. It draws inspiration from scaffold-Gaussian and OctreeGaussian for voxel registration based on gradient accumulation [S1].
+GVKF was proposed to address the issues of excessive memory consumption and rough surface details in sparse Gaussian areas inherent in discrete 3DGS representations [S1]. It was introduced at the 38th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2024) [S1]. The method builds on the fast rasterization advantages of Gaussian Splatting while integrating the effectiveness of implicit scene representations [S1]. It is used for 3D surface reconstruction in applications such as autonomous driving and virtual reality [S1].
 
